# Isolated v2 proof SQL implementation — 3 November 2026

Owned new source only: `docs/pilot/conversation/sql-drafts/p5-voice-v2-proof.sql`.
Base main: `d094ab0d1e49a845ef81225a221521ef32670deb`.

The draft installs after the base admission, v2 Auth parent/issuance and v2 cleanup drafts. It appends nullable `protocol` to the shared proof table, keeping every old row NULL and preserving the original table-rowtype `INSERT SELECT p.*` writer. Its conditional constraint permits only legacy NULL or explicit 2 with nonnil stored receipt/cleanup/claim/owner/Auth identities; existing scalar and interval constraints remain. No table, quota, accounting or Auth identity is duplicated.

Public service-only RPC: `bfa_p5_v2_register_proof(p_proof jsonb)`. Exact body and successful echo have twelve fields: protocol=2, receiptId, operation=claim|inspect, claim, cleanupId, authSessionId, parentVersion, authorityFingerprint, identityExpiresAt, verifiedAt, expiresAt, effectiveExpiresAt. The shared strict claim parser is retained, with v2 nil-ID exclusions and safe original verification/identity/proof interval checks. A successful proof lasts 1..30000 ms from its original verification, stays within all effective ceilings and is never retimed. No read or consumption RPC is added.

Any existing receipt ID—including an exact, unconsumed v2 tuple—denies before negative lifetime mutations. The ID is inserted once; success is never replayed or reattested. One receipt identifies one operation invocation; there is deliberately no uniqueness constraint on claim+operation, because later deliberate inspect operations need fresh receipts. After the shared owner advisory lock, a separate namespaced two-integer receipt advisory lock serializes v2 creators of the global receipt identity across owners. The shared unique constraint remains. Legacy writers do not participate, so quiesced cutover and native cross-backend race acceptance remain outstanding.

Private `bfa_p5_voice_v2.eligible(bfa_p5_voice.proofs)` checks the current clear exact Auth parent/version, every retained legacy session fence, current owner staging/thread/generation, immutable issuance and its protocol-2 association, exact approval and already-bound v2 cleanup. A colliding foreign or mismatched protected identity is denied before using its expiry as a shortening source. Missing data denies without creating a revocation record. No legacy/unbound cleanup is adopted. An existing shared claim must exactly match owner and full claim, remain claimed and have the matching nonretired attempt winner. Inspect requires that existing claim/winner; claim permits both absent or both exact. Orphans, retired/foreign winners and terminal state deny without repair.

All potentially waiting record locks precede retained database time. Unsafe/rollback time, future verification and future issuance deny before lifetime mutation. The minimum exact cleanup/claim, supplied effective/Auth, bounded issuance, current approval and finite allowlist deadline can only shorten the exact cleanup and matching existing claim. This negative evidence survives stale/expired proof denial. Exact current semantic invalidation (deleted or revised thread, retired/creation-mismatched generation, staging no longer admitted, revoked issuance/approval) terminally marks only the exact cleanup and matching claimed record. Elapsed effective lifetime marks them expired. No generation, attempt winner, held units, quota, capacity or provider-finality record changes. Parent/legacy binding denial does not manufacture Auth revocation.

Only successful fresh eligibility inserts a new immutable proof and echoes the exact full body. Registration neither consumes it nor grants admission or provider dispatch. A lost successful reply is uncertain committed evidence, not rollback or permission to retry with another identity. The host must keep the original request and current trusted Auth/policy provenance. The helper and private tables stay unavailable to participant/service roles directly; the one new public RPC alone receives service execution, with default new-function privileges revoked in the creation transaction.

Review-stage implementation refinement: root approved the namespaced receipt lock after identifying absent global receipt collisions across different owner locks. No behavioral failure or old-source change prompted it. No existing source, SQL, test, dependency, migration, configuration or connected service was edited by this agent.

Current source SHA256: `1c011ed347a0cd47489d2b714e583d473f45642c9f70998f3691e3bb3287c436`.
Actual SQL test results and independent review are recorded by their owning agents; root owns frozen-source full verification.

The draft remains inactive/nondeployable alone. Old proof/claim/inspect/capacity consumers still lack protocol-2 enforcement and must not receive this proof. Versioned consumption/admission/capacity, trusted host orchestration, full quiesced no-fallback cutover, deployed Auth ownership/policy/revocation and real provider/device finality are separate unfinished gates. No connected SQL, real Auth/model/voice call, activation or publication occurred.


