## Meaningful coverage

Tests install the existing P3b migrations and P5 drafts into a disposable, explicitly named offline database, then exercise the real v2 proof adapter against the new SQL. Both the seed and fixture engines are closed, and controlled SQL time is restored after every test. Assertions compare snapshots of all 22 relevant tables, excluding only exact target rows/columns where changes are allowed. Unrelated same-owner rows are therefore retained in preservation checks.

Nonzero pre-existing P3b allowance/admission, legacy cleanup/claim, quota, capacity pool/route/allocation holds are seeded. Proof registration does not create or alter reservation budgets, held amounts, attempts, capacity, or generations. Negative lifetime fencing can only shorten exact matching cleanup/claim deadlines and mark their terminal state; retained claim holds and unrelated rows remain unchanged.

Coverage includes current exact v2 parent/version, legacy-session fences, exact issuance binding, approval/thread/generation/allowlist checks, cleanup provenance and deadline restrictions, claim/attempt winner associations, terminal states, malformed input, nil identities, private helper/table access and RPC role restrictions. Claims and attempt winners seeded administratively are fictional test fixtures, not evidence of completed v2 admission.

Receipt semantics include exact immutable operation/identity tuples, no adoption of existing receipt IDs, consumed and legacy receipt collisions, no uniqueness by claim+operation, legitimate repeated inspect with distinct receipts, lost/malformed committed reply doubles, and two sequential cross-owner same-receipt orderings. Duplicate receipt denial occurs before any negative lifetime mutation. Safe/future/rollback/expiry checks preserve all protected rows; shorter valid current evidence is retained and cannot later be lengthened into renewed authority. Original proof rows remain unchanged after later close/revocation/negative observations. The existing P3b deletion fence is exercised through its real SQL function.

Schema compatibility is tested with the actual old proof writer executing before the nullable protocol column is added, preserving cached-rowtype behavior across ALTER, followed by the actual legacy register/proof/claim/inspect/close flow after ALTER. Four pre-existing legacy proof rows retain NULL protocol. Legacy mixed paths do not constitute v2 admission evidence; the old flow retains its held units and quota.

