The original completed run was **74 passed / 76 total**, with two failures during fictional fixture setup, before the tested RPC executed. The initial log `v2-proof-sql-initial.log` is preserved. One test attempted to delete a parent referenced by an issuance foreign key; it now requests an absent Auth-session identity instead. Another attempted to reference an alternate Auth session without its parent; it now creates that alternate fictional parent through the real v2 observer before testing the mismatch. No SQL implementation defect was found, and no constraint or behavioral assertion was weakened.

The initial full TypeScript check identified readonly registration-field assignments in mutable negative fixtures. A recursive mutable fixture type corrected those fixture annotations; the SQL and adapter source were unchanged by this test agent. The adapter agent's initial compiler log is separately retained as `adapter-typecheck-initial.log`.

Two cross-owner orderings were added, producing 78 passing cases in `v2-proof-sql-corrected.log`. One final assertion tightening permits only owner-clock effects for ordinary time-denial cases rather than allowing cleanup/claim effects. The final frozen JSON/log above verify that tightened assertion. An intermediate `v2-proof-sql-final.log` was empty despite an exit-0 runner and is **not used as behavioral evidence**; the explicitly captured frozen JSON/log are authoritative.

## Evidence limits

All records and approval metadata are fictional. This is local single-connection embedded PostgreSQL behavior plus response-loss/malformation doubles, not native PostgreSQL multi-connection race evidence, deployed Supabase HTTP/Auth/RLS behavior, real cryptographic approval issuance, provider/device acceptance, or runtime activation. The receipt-order cases are sequential and do not prove native lock scheduling or concurrent legacy-writer safety. Both v2 admission/capacity consumers and coordinated quiesced no-fallback cutover remain unfinished. All 21 native cases remain unrun. No connected settings, real Auth identities, model/voice/provider calls, budget spending, publication, or Lovable paid edits occurred.


